WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.490
See what people find, then decide what you stand for.

2
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.510
This is the video for module one of Personal Branding and Online Presence.

3
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:16.370
A personal brand is what people understand about you, your skills and your character when you are not in the room.

4
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.650
You already have one. Personal branding means making that picture true, clear and easy to find.

5
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:31.200
Every personal brand has three parts. Clarity: a person can say in one sentence what you do and who you help.

6
00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:39.400
Consistency: your name, photo and description match on every platform, so people know it is the same you.

7
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:46.560
Credibility: real evidence, such as photos of your work or words from satisfied clients.

8
00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:52.010
Now see yourself the way a stranger does. Set aside 30 minutes.

9
00:00:53.260 --> 00:01:02.570
Open a private or incognito tab on your phone, so your own history does not change the results. Search your full name in quotation marks.

10
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.270
Then search your name with your trade or town, and tap Images.

11
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:15.370
Open WhatsApp, Instagram, Facebook and LinkedIn, if you have them, and screenshot your profile as it looks now.

12
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:30.170
Give each platform 0 or 1 on five checks. A real, readable name. A clear photo of your face. Can a stranger tell your trade in 5 seconds? Any proof of your work? Is it clear how to reach you?

13
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:37.560
Then circle the platform that matters most for your goal and has the lowest score. That is where you start.

14
00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:45.490
For example, Amaka bakes celebration cakes in Lagos and wants orders from people outside her church circle.

15
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:53.820
On WhatsApp her name shows as Mama Tobi, the photo is her son, and her About line says Busy. She scores 1 out of 5.

16
00:01:55.070 --> 00:02:02.980
Her Instagram does better: a clear handle, 40 cake posts, and a bio. But there is no contact route. 3 out of 5.

17
00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:09.820
Her Facebook scores 1 out of 5, because the last cake post was in 2022.

18
00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:19.370
WhatsApp is where most customers message her, and it scores lowest, so she fixes it first. She keeps every screenshot in an album called Brand before.

19
00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:27.870
Next, decide what you want to be known for. Your value proposition is the reason a particular person should choose you.

20
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.210
First, who exactly do you help? Not everybody.

21
00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.700
Second, what do they struggle with or want?

22
00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:38.700
Third, what do you do about it?

23
00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:46.180
And fourth, why should they believe you? Proof must be specific and checkable, like photos of real work.

24
00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.070
Then join the four blocks into one sentence.

25
00:02:50.320 --> 00:03:00.690
For example, Halima is a bookkeeper in Kano. Her old profile said: Accountant. Hardworking and reliable. Call me. A reader learns almost nothing.

26
00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:14.050
Her new sentence: I help small shop owners in Kano know their real monthly profit, with simple bookkeeping and a one-page summary every month. Trusted by three local pharmacies.

27
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.500
And never claim clients or results you do not have.

28
00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:23.400
Two strong platforms beat six weak ones, so choose by where your audience is.

29
00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:30.000
And protect your accounts before you share your handle widely, because a public profile attracts scammers too.

30
00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:40.440
On WhatsApp, go to Settings, Account, Two-step verification, and create a six-digit PIN. Add an email address so you can reset it.

31
00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:48.260
On Instagram, Facebook and LinkedIn, open the security settings and turn on two-factor authentication.

32
00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:53.380
Use a different strong password for each account, kept somewhere safe.

33
00:03:54.630 --> 00:04:07.490
A common fraud is account takeover: someone tricks you into giving a code, then asks your contacts for money in your name. Never share a code, even with someone claiming to be the app, a bank or a friend.

34
00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:25.040
Decide now what you will never post publicly. Your home address. Your children's school or uniform. Your daily routine and live location. Photos of ID cards, bank cards or BVN details. And travel plans before you return.

35
00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.860
Share a business area, not a house address.

36
00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:41.080
Here is your practice. Run your self-search audit and score each platform. Write your positioning statement in short, medium and long versions, and test it on someone who does not know your work.

37
00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:48.130
Choose your one or two do first platforms, turn on two-step verification, and write your never-post list.

38
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.630
Now continue with the lessons below.
